1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,990
The brush uncovers a tray of coins, laid out in rows.

2
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:08,053
They are not all the same shape, metal or size, and that is the point:

3
00:00:08,053 --> 00:00:12,301
lay them in order and you have a timeline you can hold in your hand.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:24,334
This episode is about the two kinds of evidence this series has leaned on hardest, and why they are worth more than any chronicle.

5
00:00:28,767 --> 00:00:30,311
The objects themselves.

6
00:00:30,311 --> 00:00:33,079
Coins, in hoards and in excavation layers.

7
00:00:33,079 --> 00:00:37,004
Inscriptions, on rock, pillar, copper, temple wall and seal.

8
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:42,410
These are primary evidence: nobody copied them, nobody edited them centuries later,

9
00:00:42,410 --> 00:00:47,473
and most carry the date or the ruler that lets us anchor everything else.

10
00:00:51,767 --> 00:00:53,122
Follow the sequence.

11
00:00:53,122 --> 00:00:59,962
Punch-marked silver, from about the sixth century BCE, with several separate punches and no ruler's name.

12
00:00:59,962 --> 00:01:04,973
Indo-Greek coins, the first with a portrait and a name, often bilingual.

13
00:01:11,933 --> 00:01:16,055
Kushana gold, in quantity, with deities from several traditions.

14
00:01:16,055 --> 00:01:22,650
Western Kshatrapa silver, which carries dates — the most useful coins in Indian history for chronology.

15
00:01:22,650 --> 00:01:24,489
Gupta gold, in many designs.

16
00:01:24,489 --> 00:01:28,340
And the coinages of the southern and regional kingdoms.

17
00:01:30,133 --> 00:01:31,517
What a coin gives you.

18
00:01:31,517 --> 00:01:34,225
A name and a title, which gives you a ruler.

19
00:01:34,225 --> 00:01:35,849
A date, if it carries one.

20
00:01:35,849 --> 00:01:39,941
A find-spot, and enough find-spots map where a currency circulated,

21
00:01:39,941 --> 00:01:44,453
which is a better guide to influence than any claim in a praise-poem.

22
00:01:48,867 --> 00:01:54,645
The metal and its purity, which over a run of coins can indicate whether a treasury was under strain.

23
00:01:54,645 --> 00:01:58,570
And the imagery, which tells you how a ruler wanted to be seen.

24
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:03,995
Inscriptions, by type.

25
00:02:03,995 --> 00:02:06,642
Rock and pillar edicts, like Ashoka's.

26
00:02:06,642 --> 00:02:10,240
Copper-plate grants, which gave us the last episode.

27
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:14,070
Temple-wall inscriptions, which gave us the Cholas.

28
00:02:17,033 --> 00:02:21,438
Prashastis, praise-poems like the Allahabad and Aihole inscriptions.

29
00:02:21,438 --> 00:02:21,884
Seals.

30
00:02:21,884 --> 00:02:23,225
And memorial stones.

31
00:02:23,225 --> 00:02:29,716
Each type was made for a different purpose, and knowing the purpose is how you read it properly.

32
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:36,699
And the decipherment, without which none of this would be readable.

33
00:02:36,699 --> 00:02:41,824
James Prinsep worked out Brahmi in 1837, and Kharoshthi was read in the same period.

34
00:02:41,824 --> 00:02:50,322
Before that, centuries of Indian history were literally illegible — the stones were standing in plain sight and nobody could read them.

35
00:02:52,567 --> 00:02:56,453
Dated inscriptions are what anchor Indian chronology:

36
00:02:56,453 --> 00:03:05,101
eras like the Shaka era from 78 CE and the Gupta era from 319 or 320 CE let a dated stone fix everything around it.

37
00:03:08,033 --> 00:03:09,799
Three terms worth having.

38
00:03:09,799 --> 00:03:12,176
Numismatics is the study of coins.

39
00:03:12,176 --> 00:03:15,193
Epigraphy is the study of inscriptions.

40
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,418
Palaeography is the study of old handwriting and letter forms — which is how an undated inscription can still be roughly dated,

41
00:03:23,418 --> 00:03:25,172
by the shape of its letters.

42
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:31,916
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

43
00:03:31,916 --> 00:03:34,340
Coins date it; inscriptions place it.

44
00:03:34,340 --> 00:03:37,703
Between them they hold up the whole chronology.

45
00:03:43,033 --> 00:03:43,789
Dig Quiz.

46
00:03:43,789 --> 00:03:45,223
Pause and name it.

47
00:03:45,223 --> 00:03:52,322
First: the earliest Indian coinage, silver with several separate punches and no ruler's name.

48
00:03:52,322 --> 00:03:54,133
Punch-marked coins.

49
00:03:56,867 --> 00:03:59,270
Second: the study of inscriptions.

50
00:03:59,270 --> 00:04:00,025
Epigraphy.

51
00:04:00,025 --> 00:04:06,505
Third: the study of old letter forms, which lets an undated inscription be roughly dated.

52
00:04:11,033 --> 00:04:11,957
Palaeography.

53
00:04:11,957 --> 00:04:18,851
In Mains, a frequent question asks why archaeological sources are more reliable than literary ones.

54
00:04:20,633 --> 00:04:23,535
Give the reason precisely: they are contemporary,

55
00:04:23,535 --> 00:04:30,336
unedited and often dated — but add that they are also partial, since they record what somebody wanted recorded.

56
00:04:34,367 --> 00:04:39,195
A tray of coins takes its place in the gallery, a thousand years laid out in rows.

57
00:04:42,133 --> 00:04:46,749
Next gallery: a subject where the sources are thinner, more prescriptive,

58
00:04:46,749 --> 00:04:51,303
and almost entirely written by men — and how to read it honestly anyway.

59
00:04:51,303 --> 00:04:55,159
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

